<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-coding-workflow">
  <title>Coding Workflow</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Coding is a sequence of decisions, not one judgment that a response <q>looks
      right</q> or <q>seems wrong.</q> First record the evidence present in the
      response. Then represent that evidence as one or more episodes, apply the
      permitted codebook resources, evaluate the mathematics, and document any
      uncertainty.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-coding-before">
    <title>Before Coding</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-context">
      <title>Read the Question Context</title>

      <p>
        When coding, begin with the exact question and identify:
        <ul>
          <li>the information supplied to the student;</li>
          <li>the mathematical objects and representations shown;</li>
          <li>the conclusion, explanation, or computation requested;</li>
          <li>each distinct goal the response is expected to address; and</li>
          <li>the agreed mathematical state and 
            <xref ref="g-valid-link-set" text="custom">valid-inference set</xref>
            for the task.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A phrase can support different judgments in different contexts. For example,
        <q>a free variable exists</q> rules out a unique solution when the system is known
        to be consistent, but it does not by itself establish that any solution exists.
        The question context determines what the criterion means and which inference is
        valid.
      </p>

      <p>
        Keep the question, reference mathematics, listed goals, and working codebook
        available throughout the pass unless the study design deliberately hides one of
        them.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-versions">
      <title>Confirm Governing Versions</title>
      <p>
        Every coding record must be interpretable later. Before coding, confirm the
        versions of the materials that govern the pass. At minimum, record:
        <ul>
          <li>the handbook or workflow version;</li>
          <li>the Coding Protocol <m>\pi</m> version;</li>
          <li>the codebook version, when the codebook is available;</li>
          <li>the question-specific working-codebook version or identifier; and</li>
          <li>the controlled-vocabulary version when vocabulary normalization is used.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the versions assigned for the batch, even if a newer draft appears during
        coding. A rule that seems inadequate should be flagged for later revision; it
        should not be silently changed halfway through the batch. Otherwise, apparent
        coder disagreement may actually reflect different rules.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-independent-pass">
      <title>Protect the Independent Pass</title>
      <p>
        Independent passes must follow the access rules stated in the assigned
        worksheet. Do not use unauthorized materials. We need to preserve the 
        independent record before beginning an assisted, comparison, or review pass.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-coding-pass">
    <title>The Coding Pass</title>
    <p>
      Complete the following steps in order. Later steps depend on earlier ones, and a
      disagreement should be located at the earliest step where two analyses diverge.
      Do not let a likely code determine the episode structure, and do not let a correct
      final answer excuse a weak inference.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-screen">
      <title>Step 1: Screen</title>

      <p>
        Screening asks whether the response contains analyzable justification. It does
        not ask whether the response is mathematically correct. An incorrect explanation
        may be codable, while a correct bare answer may contain no codable justification.
      </p>

      <p>
        Read the entire response and record a screening decision with a brief rationale.
        Continue to episode analysis when the response contains, or plausibly contains,
        a mathematical criterion connected to a judgment. Preserve uncertain cases for
        review rather than excluding them merely because the reasoning is unfamiliar or
        difficult to parse.
      </p>

      <p>
        Blank responses, bare answers, question restatements, and operation-only work
        require the specific rules in <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>.
        At this stage, record what is present; do not infer the most likely justification
        from the answer.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-segment">
      <title>Step 2: Segment</title>

      <p>
        Segmentation determines how many local criterion-to-judgment moves appear and
        where their boundaries fall. Work from the response itself before consulting
        likely codes. Connector words such as <q>so</q>, <q>because</q>, <q>since</q>, and
        <q>therefore</q> are useful signals, but they are not definitions of episode
        boundaries.
      </p>

      <p>
        Use one episode candidate for each distinct move in which a cited feature
        supports a judgment. The written order may differ from the analytic order:
        <q><m>J</m> because <m>C</m></q> still represents
        <m>C\rightarrow J</m>. A single sentence may contain several chained moves, and
        several sentences may elaborate one move.
      </p>

      <p>
        The current nesting-retention policy remains open in the source materials.
        Until a later protocol supersedes the record-everything rule, preserve every
        identifiable episode, mark its relation, and note any defensible alternative
        segmentation. Do not collapse a possible sub-step merely to simplify the sheet.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-transcribe">
      <title>Step 3: Record Criterion and Judgment</title>

      <p>
        For each episode candidate, record the criterion and judgment before writing the
        inferential link. Use the student's own words in fields that require verbatim
        evidence.
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Criterion</title>
            <p>
              Copy the words, symbols, or stated result that function as the reason. Do not
              import a theorem, condition, or intermediate fact the student did not name.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Judgment</title>
            <p>
              Copy the conclusion, classification, or decision the student draws. Do not
              replace it with the answer the student should have given.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
      <p>
        A criterion without a judgment and a judgment without a criterion are preserved
        as incomplete evidence. Missing information remains missing. Record
        <c>complete = 1</c> only when both a criterion and a judgment are identifiable;
        otherwise record <c>complete = 0</c>. An absent inferential link alone does not
        make the episode incomplete.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-link">
      <title>Step 4: Record the Inferential Link</title>
      <p>
        Record the minimal link: the smallest justification supported by the response
        that suffices to connect the criterion to the judgment. The purpose is to
        represent the student's justification, not to write a complete textbook proof.
      </p>

      <p>
        Assign one evidence status:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title><c>explicit</c></title>
            <p>The connection is directly stated or clearly expressed in the wording.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title><c>implicit</c></title>
            <p>
              The connection is unstated but recoverable without adding a new
              mathematical reason.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title><c>absent</c></title>
            <p>No inferential link can be recovered without guessing or repairing.</p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Connector words are evidence of a connection, not evidence that the connection
        is valid. Conversely, the absence of a connector does not force
        <c>link_stated = absent</c> when the relationship is otherwise clear. When no
        link is recoverable, enter the project's designated missing-link value, such as
        <c>[none stated]</c>, and preserve the complete episode if both criterion and
        judgment are still present.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-relation">
      <title>Step 5: Record Episode Relations</title>

      <p>
        When a response contains more than one episode, record how the episodes relate.
        Use the definitions in <xref ref="sec-episode-relations" text="title"/>.
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Chained</title>
            <p>
              The judgment of one episode becomes the criterion of another. Identify the
              earlier episode, for example <c>chained(1)</c>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Independent</title>
            <p>
              Separate episodes provide distinct justifications without one depending on
              the other.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Nested</title>
            <p>
              One episode functions as a sub-step within a larger episode or argument.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Unresolved</title>
            <p>
              More than one relation or segmentation is defensible under the current
              evidence or policy.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Episode existence and retention policy are separate decisions. Preserve the
        visible structure first. A later uniform policy may determine whether parent and
        child episodes are both included in a particular analysis.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-match">
      <title>Step 6: Match the Codebook</title>

      <p>
        Perform this step only when the assigned work mode permits codebook access.
        Compare each episode with the frozen question-specific working codebook. Consider
        the code definition, inclusion and exclusion rules, positive and near-miss
        examples, confusable codes, and the question-specific criterion-to-judgment
        relationship.
      </p>

      <p>
        Do not assign a code from an isolated keyword. A response that mentions pivots
        may actually rely on dimension, consistency, or a comparison between the number
        of pivots and variables. Code the role the idea plays in the episode.
      </p>

      <p>
        Record the best-supported code in <c>code</c>. When more than one code plausibly
        fits the same episode, preserve runner-up codes in <c>alt_codes</c> and explain
        the ambiguity. Distinguish this from a response containing several distinct
        episodes, each of which may receive its own code.
      </p>

      <p>
        Do not force a nearest match. Record an <term>OOV</term> case when relevant
        student language is not represented in the controlled vocabulary. Record a
        <term>no-match</term> case when the episode is representable but no current
        codebook entry describes the control adequately. The detailed diagnostic
        process appears in <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-correctness">
      <title>Step 7: Evaluate Correctness</title>

      <p>
        Evaluate correctness only after the episode has been identified. For each
        complete episode, assign
        <md>
          \operatorname{code}(e_k)=(c_k^1,c_k^2),
        </md>
        using the normative reference for the exact question.
        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>Criterion correctness, <m>c_k^1</m></title>
            <p>
              Record 1 when the criterion is true in the actual mathematical state and 0
              when it is false.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Inferential validity, <m>c_k^2</m></title>
            <p>
              Record 1 when the criterion validly supports the judgment for this question
              and 0 when it does not.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
      <p>
        Score the two components independently. A true criterion followed by an invalid
        inference is the important case <m>(1,0)</m>, not a vaguely <q>mostly correct</q>
        response. Leave both digits blank for an incomplete episode; blank is not the
        same as <m>(0,0)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A score of <m>(1,1)</m> certifies the local justification, not the complete
        answer. Do not change an episode's correctness code because the response omits
        another requested conclusion.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-goal-coverage">
      <title>Step 8: Evaluate Goal Coverage</title>

      <p>
        Compare the response's judgments with the goals listed in the question-specific
        frame. Record which goals each episode addresses and which requested outputs
        remain uncovered. Use <c>off-target</c> when a judgment addresses none of the
        listed goals and <c>unclear</c>, with a note, when its relation to the goals cannot
        be determined.
      </p>

      <p>
        Goal coverage is not a reason to discard an episode. A response may contain a
        mathematically sound local episode while failing to answer the full question.
        Conversely, it may state the requested final answer through an invalid inference.
        Preserve both facts separately.
      </p>

      <p>
        The source materials do not yet define one final response-level aggregation rule
        for episode goal-coverage annotations. Until such a rule is adopted, record the
        episode-level coverage and the response-level missing goals explicitly rather
        than inventing a summary score.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-confidence">
      <title>Step 9: Record Confidence and Rationale</title>

      <p>
        Confidence describes the coding decision, not the student's mathematical
        correctness. Use the project's three-point scale:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title><c>3</c></title>
            <p><term>Highly confident</term>. The reading is clear and there is no plausible alternative.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title><c>2</c></title>
            <p>
              <term>Fairly confident</term>. The reading is clear, but another coder could reasonably
              interpret the case differently.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title><c>1</c></title>
            <p>
              <term>Unsure</term>. The reading is unclear or ambiguous.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        A number without an explanation is weak data. For every low-confidence decision,
        name the source of uncertainty: screening, boundary, criterion, link, judgment,
        relation, matching, correctness, coverage, vocabulary, or policy. Never leave a
        <c>confidence = 1</c> row without a note.
      </p>

      <p>
        Write rationales that are brief, specific, and tied to visible evidence. Useful
        forms include:
        <ul>
          <li><q>Included because the response states a criterion and a judgment connected by <q>so.</q></q></li>
          <li><q>The judgment is missing; the response states only that a free variable exists.</q></li>
          <li><q>Two episodes are recorded because the first judgment becomes the second criterion.</q></li>
          <li><q>No code assigned because the episode relies on dimension rather than the mentioned pivot term.</q></li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Avoid claims about what the student <q>understands</q> or <q>must have meant.</q>
        Describe the response and the coding decision.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-coding-route">
      <title>Step 10: Route or Escalate</title>

      <p>
        Finish by recording the disposition required by the current review policy. The
        source materials use dispositions such as ordinary review, terminology review,
        no-match review, and escalation; computational materials also include labels such
        as <c>auto_accept</c> and <c>standard_review</c>. Use the exact allowed values and
        triggers supplied for the batch rather than inventing a new label.
      </p>

      <p>
        Escalate or flag a case when the current evidence or rules do not support a
        defensible ordinary decision. Common reasons include:
        <ul>
          <li>uncertain screening or episode boundaries;</li>
          <li>competing interpretations of a criterion, link, or judgment;</li>
          <li>nested reasoning affected by an unresolved retention policy;</li>
          <li>multiple plausible code matches;</li>
          <li>OOV or no-match reasoning;</li>
          <li>insufficient normative information for correctness;</li>
          <li>an apparent contradiction in the question or protocol; or</li>
          <li>a recurring case that may require a codebook or guidance revision.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        State what decision is blocked and what evidence would resolve it. An unresolved
        case is a legitimate research record. It is preferable to a confident-looking
        code that the evidence does not support.
      </p>

      <p>
        In a tool-review pass, preserve both the computational proposal and
        the human action or override. The assigned review worksheet or tool
        documentation supplies the allowed action vocabulary and required
        fields; this chapter governs the evidence-first review principle.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-coding-sheet">
    <title>Coding Sheet Reference</title>
    <p>
      Use one row per episode. A response with several episodes receives several
      episode rows, while a response with no complete episode still requires a
      response-level record showing what was observed and how it was screened.
    </p>
    <p>
      Complete only the fields authorized by the assigned worksheet. The
      authoritative data dictionary is
      <xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/>; the worksheet identifies which
      fields are available, required, or withheld for the current milestone.
    </p>
    <remark xml:id="rem-coding-no-silent-revision">
      <title>Do Not Silently Revise Earlier Rows</title>
      <p>
        When you discover that your interpretation has drifted, do not quietly 
        rewrite earlier rows so that the original coding process disappears.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-coding-checklist">
    <title>Final Checklist</title>

    <p>
      Before submitting a record or batch, confirm:
      <ol>
      <li><p>I used the assigned question context and governing versions.</p></li>
      <li><p>I recorded evidence before interpretation.</p></li>
      <li><p>I did not repair missing reasoning.</p></li>
      <li><p>I preserved ambiguity, alternatives, and uncertainty.</p></li>
      <li><p>I completed only fields authorized for this milestone.</p></li>
      <li><p>I did not overwrite an earlier independent or proposed record.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      Milestone-specific completion and submission checks belong to the assigned
      worksheet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      A defensible coding record shows not only the final code but how the researcher
      reached it. The workflow preserves the original response, separates episode
      extraction from codebook matching, distinguishes mathematical truth from
      inferential validity, and records where uncertainty remains. These features allow
      later <xref ref="g-calibration" text="title"/>, 
      <xref ref="g-adjudication" text="title"/>, tool comparison, and methodological 
      revision without reconstructing the coder's decisions from memory.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</chapter>